From 3598bc3a4bf0bba22a3b3824f223fc1905c5c4de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 09:23:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [GStreamer] Apply the sample range of YUV video frames

https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=330300

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

The YUV layer buffer converted every frame as if the sample range were
fixed by the matrix. The Skia path used the full-range SkYUVColorSpace
for BT.709, BT.2020 and SMPTE 240M and the limited-range one for BT.601,
and the TextureMapper path used limited-range matrices throughout.
Broadcast and streaming BT.709 is limited range, so on the Skia path
black was raised and white stopped near 235, while full-range BT.601,
such as MJPEG camera video, was stretched and clipped at both ends.

Carry the range from GstVideoColorimetry to the layer buffer through
VideoFrameGStreamer::yuvColorimetry() and DMABufBuffer::SampleRange, and
apply it in both renderers. Skia selects the limited or full variant of
each SkYUVColorSpace, and the 16-bit BT.2020 variant for P010, which
keeps its 10-bit codes in the top bits of 16-bit samples. TextureMapper
derives its matrix from Kr, Kb and the range equations of ITU-T H.273
for 8-bit samples and for P010; this reproduces the limited-range
tables it used for BT.601, BT.709 and BT.2020, and takes Kr 0.212 and
Kb 0.087 for SMPTE 240M as H.273, GStreamer and Skia do.

